course: biology
lesson: 327
title: A kitten at home: litter trays, scratching posts and play
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A kitten is a tiny hunter in training. Every pounce on a string, every ambush of your toes under the duvet is practice for the stalking and catching a wild cat would need.
explain: Most of its strange habits have good reasons. It buries its poo so enemies cannot smell it, scratches to clean its claws and leave scent, and kneads your jumper the way it once kneaded its mother to make the milk flow.
explain: Picture a kitten halfway up the curtains, mewing: its claws curve backwards like hooks, so they grip well going up but not coming down head first. Left calm, it usually works out how to back down, like a person down a ladder.
explain: A good home gives a kitten a clean tray, a tall steady post, safe toys instead of string or hands, high places to escape a dog, and indoor life until it is vaccinated, neutered (given a small operation at the vet's so it cannot have kittens) and old enough.
fact: Cats bury their poo by instinct, and kittens learn the tray by watching their mother; a common rule is one tray for each cat plus one extra.
fact: Scratching pulls off old outer layers of the claws and leaves scent from glands in the paws, so a tall, steady scratching post is needed.
fact: String, thread and ribbon must never be left lying about, because a kitten can swallow them and get badly hurt inside; wand toys with feathers are safer for play with a grown-up.
fact: A kitten pouncing on feet under a duvet is practising stalking hidden prey, and using toys instead of hands and feet stops it learning to bite people.
fact: All kittens are born with blue eyes, their eyes open at about one to two weeks, and their real eye colour shows from about six or seven weeks.
fact: Most cats have 18 toes, five on each front paw and four on each back paw, and cats walk on their toes.
fact: Some cats have extra toes, and Ernest Hemingway's old house in Florida still has dozens of cats, many with six toes.
fact: A kitten has 26 baby teeth and a grown cat has 30, and the baby teeth fall out from about three to four months.
fact: Cat claws curve backwards, so they grip well going up but not going down head first, and cats come down trees backwards.
fact: Kittens stay inside until they are vaccinated, neutered and old enough, because outside there are cars, dogs and fights with other cats.
fact: Kittens knead their mother's tummy to make her milk flow, and many cats keep kneading all their lives when happy; cats purr when content but sometimes also when hurt or scared.
fact: A kitten and a dog should meet slowly, first by smell and through a gate, and the kitten needs high places to escape to.
exercise: Why does a kitten cover its poo in the tray? | Cats bury their poo by instinct | It is ashamed of what it did and hides it | It is looking for some buried food | It wants to build a cosy nest there
exercise: How many litter trays does the common rule suggest for two cats? | Three | Just one, shared | Two, one each | Four or more
exercise: Why does a kitten scratch things? | To pull off old claw layers and leave its scent | To show the family it is hungry and wants its dinner | To sharpen its teeth | To dig for water
exercise: What should a scratching post be like? | Tall and steady | Short and wobbly | Soft like a pillow | Made of thin glass
exercise: Why must string never be left lying around a kitten? | It can swallow it and get badly hurt inside | It makes kittens grow too fast and stretch too thin | String makes kittens sneeze | Kittens are scared of string
exercise: Why does a kitten pounce on feet under the duvet? | It is practising stalking hidden prey | It wants to sleep in the shoes | It thinks the feet are its mother | It is cold and wants to dig in
exercise: What colour are all kittens' eyes when they first open? | Blue | Green | Yellow | Brown
exercise: When does a kitten's real eye colour start to show? | From about six or seven weeks | Never, it stays blue for the whole of its life | Right at birth, when it is born | At about five years old, when grown
exercise: How many toes does a kitten usually have on each front paw and each back paw? | Five on each front paw and four on each back paw | Four on every paw | Five on every paw | Four on each front paw and five on each back paw
exercise: Whose old house in Florida has dozens of cats, many with six toes? | Ernest Hemingway's | Walt Disney's | Charles Darwin's | Isaac Newton's
exercise: How many teeth does a grown cat have? | 30 | 26 | 42 | 18
exercise: Why can a kitten climb up curtains but not get down? | Its claws curve backwards and grip only going up | It forgets how it got up there and is too scared to look | Its legs get worn out from the long climb to the top | Curtains are far too slippery for its claws going up
exercise: How do cats get down from trees? | Backwards, like a person down a ladder | Head first, running fast | They always wait for firefighters | They jump from the very top
exercise: Why does a young kitten stay inside? | Until it is vaccinated, neutered and old enough, the street is dangerous | Outside air turns kittens' eyes blue, so they must stay in until their eyes change | Kittens hate fresh air | Kittens cannot walk on grass
exercise: Why do kittens knead their mother's tummy? | To make her milk flow | To push her away when they are full | To keep her awake all day | To clean and brush her fur
exercise: How should a new kitten meet the family dog? | Slowly, first by smell and through a gate | Put them together in a box at once | Let the dog chase it to make friends | Never, cats and dogs cannot live together
test: Count them: a kitten has 26 baby teeth and a grown cat has 30. How many more does the grown cat have? | 4 | 6 | 16 | 2
test: Count them: a Hemingway cat has one extra toe on every paw. How many toes in all? | 22 | 18 | 24 | 20
test: Count them: Tom chases Jerry round the kitchen. If Tom were a real cat, how many toes on his four paws? | 18 | 20 | 16 | 10
test: In Tom and Jerry, Tom chases Jerry with a hammer and gets flattened, then pops back. What does a real kitten do when it chases? | Practises the stalking and pouncing of a hunter, with paws and claws only | Uses little tools, like sticks and string, to trap mice and pull them out | Uses tools to catch mice | Only chases cheese
test: In a cartoon a kitten plays with a ball of wool all afternoon alone. What is the safer truth? | Wool and string can be swallowed and hurt a kitten; play with a wand toy and a grown-up | Wool is the best toy for any kitten, and kittens can digest a bit of wool with no trouble | Kittens can digest wool easily | Wool keeps kittens' teeth clean
test: «kitten's stuck up the curtains, call the fire brigade!» What is the best first step? | Stay calm and give it time; kittens usually back down | Shake the curtains hard so that it lets go and drops down | Pull it off by the tail | Throw a pillow at it
test: «my kitten's got blue eyes so it'll stay blue forever» What is the truth? | All kittens start with blue eyes; the real colour shows from about six or seven weeks | Yes, blue at birth means blue for life | Eyes go blue only when kittens are sad | Only white kittens have blue eyes
test: «the cat's purring so she must be happy, even tho she's hurt her leg» What is right? | Cats purr when content but sometimes also when hurt, so she may need a vet | Purring always means total happiness, so a purring cat cannot be badly hurt | Purring always means total happiness | Purring heals any leg in a day
test: What would happen if the only place a kitten could scratch were the sofa? | It would scratch the sofa, because it needs to scratch somewhere | It would stop scratching for ever, since there was nowhere it was allowed | Its claws would fall off | It would scratch only the dog
test: «why does my cat knead my jumper like dough» What is the best answer? | It is an old kitten habit from feeding, done when it is relaxed | It is checking for mice hiding inside the jumper, ready to pounce | It wants to bake bread | It is angry at the jumper
test: A kitten arrives in a house with a dog. Why does it need high shelves? | So it can escape up out of the dog's reach | So it can see the television | Kittens sleep only on shelves | To hide its food
exam: Identify the reason for which kittens knead with their forepaws. | The behaviour originates in stimulating the flow of the mother's milk | The behaviour is a signal of hunger for meat and is directed at the owner | The behaviour serves to sharpen the teeth | The behaviour is learnt from dogs
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the eye colour of newborn kittens. | All kittens possess blue eyes initially, and the definitive colour appears from approximately six to seven weeks | Kittens possess green eyes at birth which become blue | The eye colour of a kitten never alters | Kittens are born with their eyes open and brown
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the difficulty of a cat in descending a tree? | Its claws curve backwards and grip effectively only during ascent | Its claws are retracted entirely during descent, so that it cannot grip the bark | It is unable to see the ground from a height | It is afraid of the bark of the tree
exam: Ascertain the danger presented to a kitten by string, thread or ribbon. | Such items may be swallowed and cause serious internal injury | Such items induce sneezing | Such items cause the claws to fall out | Such items alter the colour of the eyes
